      "writtenOn": "2026-04-25T12:40:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I think error may be better, as a behave correct host should unmap the protected memory from its MMU (from its own safety point of view, as I mentioned at https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/kernel/common/+/4047493/comments/d96f53d5_03212499), thus the load_unaligned_zeropad() case should not cause EPT violation but #PF.\n\nIdeally yes, and hopefully some day (when we switch to guest_memfd) that will be the case, but currently it is not the case, since aosp/4047493 covers only reserved pKVM memory, not donated memory? i.e. load_unaligned_zeropad() _will_ cause EPT violation when it crosses a donated page.\n\nBTW I suppose it is actually more likely for load_unaligned_zeropad() to stumble across a donated page than a reserved pKVM page, since reserved pKVM memory is a single contiguous chunk of physical memory, while donated pages are arbitrarily spread across memory?\n\nAnyway, it is not so important to me if we change it to warning or not, I\u0027m fine with keeping it as an error (after all, it is only printed in the debug mode anyway).\n\n\u003e and this may also potentially cause conflict in the future if this non-standard hardware bit is used for other purpose, so if this is just to let the host to print some error log, sounds a bit overkill?\n\nYeah, after more thinking I agree with that. Basically this error logging is a nice-to-have.\n\nI was just a bit worried that we don\u0027t have any logging in dmesg (in non-debug mode) that would indicate that the given #PF is due to the host trying to access pKVM-protected memory, not just a regular page fault due to some regular kernel bug, so some extra (possibly difficult) debugging work would be needed to find that out.\n\nSo, still, if we could implement some (easy and safe) trick to somehow convey this information to the host, it would be nice. Not necessarily via the #PF error code, maybe via some other part of the host vCPU state?\n\nI was thinking, for example, about setting some special bit in the fault address in CR2, but I\u0027m not sure that would work either. But we also have a bunch of other registers?\n\nFor comparison, pKVM-ARM achieves that by setting a bit which, IIUC, should only be set by HW in EL2, not in EL1, so if the host kernel in EL1 detects that this bit is set, it knows it is non-architectural and thus it must have been set by pKVM, not by hardware: https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/kernel/common/+/3864576/14/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/mem_protect.c#625",
      "parentUuid": "7dcc02d1_652acfb9",
      "revId": "f1734cc1f96f0774946220f7c1c3e5377060df43",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
